<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiram Bingham III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiram Bingham III was an American academic, explorer and politician who in 1911 led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> expedition that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> to international attention. On the same journey he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a> in search of Vilcabamba, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> refuge. He later served as a military aviator in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and US senator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiram-bingham-iii.jpg" alt="Hiram Bingham III" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Photo Company Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Senator_Hiram_Bingham_of_Conn.,_(1-9-25)_LCCN2016839105_(3x4a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
